  "vocal_prose_script": "Imagine a coin that is either regular (heads on one side, tails on the other) or fraudulent (heads on both sides). Classically, you must inspect both sides. Deutsch-Jozsa uses quantum interference to test the global property across all inputs simultaneously with a single query.",
  "mathematical_latex_formula": "|\\psi_3\\rangle = \\sum_{x \\in \\{0,1\\}^n} \\left( \\sum_{z \\in \\{0,1\\}^n} (-1)^{x \\cdot z + f(x)} \\right) |z\\rangle,\\quad P(0^{\\otimes n}) = 1 \\iff f \\text{ is constant}",
  "qiskit_executable_code": "from qiskit import QuantumCircuit\n# 2-input Deutsch-Jozsa (constant oracle)\nqc = QuantumCircuit(3, 2)\nqc.x(2); qc.h([0,1,2])\n# Identity oracle (f(x)=0 constant)\nqc.h([0,1])\nqc.measure([0,1], [0,1])",
  "quiz": {
    "question": "If measuring all input qubits after Deutsch-Jozsa produces |00...0>, what is the function?",
    "options": [
      "Guaranteed constant",
      "Guaranteed balanced",
      "50% chance of either",
      "Indeterminate without ancilla readout"
    ],
    "answer": 0,
    "explanation": "Constructive interference on the all-zero state |0...0> occurs if and only if f(x) is constant. If balanced, destructive interference cancels |0...0> completely."
  },
  "sources": [
    "Deutsch & Jozsa \u2013 Rapid Solution of Problems by Quantum Computation (Proc. R. Soc. Lond. 1992)"
  ]
}

