  "intent_classification": "phase_kickback_mechanism",
  "vocal_prose_script": "Imagine pushing against a heavy stone wall while standing on roller skates: instead of the wall moving, the push kicks you backward. In phase kickback, when a controlled gate acts on an eigenvector of the target qubit, the resulting eigenvalue phase is kicked back onto the control qubit.",
  "mathematical_latex_formula": "U|u\\rangle = e^{i\\theta}|u\\rangle \\implies C(U)\\left( \\frac{|0\\rangle + |1\\rangle}{\\sqrt{2}} \\otimes |u\\rangle \\right) = \\frac{|0\\rangle + e^{i\\theta}|1\\rangle}{\\sqrt{2}} \\otimes |u\\rangle",
  "qiskit_executable_code": "from qiskit import QuantumCircuit\nqc = QuantumCircuit(2)\nqc.h(0)  # Control qubit in |+>\nqc.x(1); qc.h(1)  # Target qubit in |-> (eigenstate of X with eigenvalue -1)\nqc.cx(0, 1)  # Phase -1 kicks back to control qubit q0\nqc.h(0)  # q0 becomes |1>",
  "quiz": {
    "question": "What state must the target qubit be in for phase kickback to occur with a unitary operator U?",
    "options": [
      "An eigenstate |u> of U such that U|u> = e^(i theta)|u>",
      "The computational ground state |0> exclusively",
      "A maximally entangled Bell state with the control qubit",
      "Any mixed state with non-zero trace distance"
    ],
    "answer": 0,
    "explanation": "Phase kickback relies directly on the target being an eigenvector |u> of U. The eigenvalue e^(i theta) factors out and modifies the relative phase of the control qubit."
  },
  "sources": [
    "Cleve, Ekert, Macchiavello, Mosca \u2013 Quantum Algorithms Revisited (Proc. R. Soc. 1998)"
  ]
}

